Presence, not content.

GitHub Pages runs Jekyll over a published tree unless this file exists at its
root, and Jekyll excludes every path beginning with an underscore -- which is the
whole of _next/. The failure mode is not a 404 page: the HTML is served fine and
every stylesheet and script inside it is missing, so the site renders as unstyled
text. That looks like a CSS bug and is not one.

The artifact-based Pages deployment this repo uses (actions/upload-pages-artifact
plus actions/deploy-pages) does not invoke Jekyll, so today this file is
insurance rather than a load-bearing part of the build. It stays because the
protection costs nothing and the failure it prevents is both total and
misdiagnosable, and because a switch back to branch-based publishing would
otherwise reintroduce it silently.

apps/web-v2/public/.nojekyll is the same file for the same reason.
